-- title:
--   A third bidegree-(2,2) addition law on Weierstrass projective models
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring and $W$ a Weierstrass curve over $R$, with projective defining polynomial $W.toProjective.polynomial$ in $R[X_0,X_1,X_2]$. Working in $R[\,X_{\mathrm{inl}\,0},X_{\mathrm{inl}\,1},X_{\mathrm{inl}\,2},X_{\mathrm{inr}\,0},X_{\mathrm{inr}\,1},X_{\mathrm{inr}\,2}]$ — the coordinate ring of a pair of points, the two copies of the curve equation being $WP_1$ and $WP_2$, the images of the projective polynomial under the two renamings — the module writes down three explicit polynomials `kw_lrThird_X`, `kw_lrThird_Y`, `kw_lrThird_Z`. Each is a sum of terms $C(c)\cdot m_{\mathrm l}\cdot m_{\mathrm r}$ in which $m_{\mathrm l}$ and $m_{\mathrm r}$ are monomials of degree $2$ in the left and in the right triple of variables respectively, and $c$ is an explicit polynomial in $a_1,a_2,a_3,a_4,a_6$; so the triple is bihomogeneous of bidegree $(2,2)$, and the three accompanying lemmas record homogeneity of total degree $4$.
--
--   Two further groups of results relate this triple to the data of the imported module. First, `kw_lrThird_pXZ`, `kw_lrThird_qXZ`, `kw_lrThird_pYZ`, `kw_lrThird_qYZ` are explicit cofactors, and `kw_lrThird_minor_XZ` and `kw_lrThird_minor_YZ` are the polynomial identities
--   $$T_XA_Z-T_ZA_X=p_{XZ}WP_1+q_{XZ}WP_2,\qquad T_YA_Z-T_ZA_Y=p_{YZ}WP_1+q_{YZ}WP_2,$$
--   where $(A_X,A_Y,A_Z)=($`kw_lrAdd_X`$,$ `kw_lrAdd_Y`$,$ `kw_lrAdd_Z`$)$ is the chord triple of the imported module; the companion statements `kw_lrThird_minor_XZ_mem_span` and `kw_lrThird_minor_YZ_mem_span` restate these as membership of the two $2\times2$ minors in the ideal generated by $WP_1$ and $WP_2$, i.e. proportionality of the two triples modulo the pair of curve equations. Second, `kw_lrThird_X_diag`, `kw_lrThird_Y_diag`, `kw_lrThird_Z_diag` state that substituting the same triple of variables for both the left and the right variables (the algebra map $\delta$ given by `aeval (Sum.elim X X)`) carries $T_X,T_Y,T_Z$ to the doubling polynomials `kw_lrSym_dblX`, `kw_lrSym_dblY`, `kw_lrSym_dblZ` — so unlike the chord and symmetric laws this triple restricts on the diagonal to the doubling triple itself rather than to a multiple of it.
--
--   **Relation to Mathlib.** Mathlib supplies the Weierstrass curve structure and its projective defining polynomial (`WeierstrassCurve.toProjective`, `WeierstrassCurve.Projective.polynomial`) together with the homogeneity API for multivariate polynomials; the bidegree-$(2,2)$ addition-law triples, their cofactors and the minor identities are the project's own.
--
--   **Where it is used.** Together with the chord triple and the symmetric triple of the imported module, this triple provides an addition law on the projective Weierstrass model that is defined along the diagonal, so that addition and doubling are given by universal polynomial formulae over an arbitrary base ring.
